cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you reference irlume in academic or security work, please cite it as below."
title: "irlume: infrared face authentication for Linux (PAM)"
abstract: >-
  irlume is a Linux PAM face-authentication daemon that uses a paired
  infrared and RGB camera for a Windows Hello-style credential: it seals the
  login secret in the TPM under a measured-boot policy and releases it only on
  a live infrared face match. Templates are stored as 512-D embeddings, never
  images; the primary enrollment store is encrypted at rest (AES-256-GCM
  under a TPM-sealed key; secondary multi-camera stores are root-only
  plaintext today). Anti-spoofing ships two learned PAD models on by default
  (ViT on RGB, FLIR IR on the infrared path) alongside algorithmic infrared
  gates (reflectance and centre/edge relief ratio, with corneal glint
  recorded as a supporting cue).
type: software
